WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.376 --> 00:00:04.209
- What's up Believe Nation? It's Evan.

00:00:04.209 --> 00:00:06.127
My one word is believe,

00:00:06.127 --> 00:00:08.171
and I believe that you have the ability

00:00:08.171 --> 00:00:09.634
to create something special

00:00:09.634 --> 00:00:11.288
that will help change the planet.

00:00:11.288 --> 00:00:12.580
So to help you in your journey,

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:14.072
I started the Mentor Me series,

00:00:14.072 --> 00:00:16.217
and the goal here is to
try to hang around people

00:00:16.217 --> 00:00:17.784
who have done a lot more than us,

00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:19.819
who achieve massive success,

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:22.297
and hopefully by hanging around
them a little bit longer,

00:00:22.297 --> 00:00:24.970
some of their mindsets
or attitudes or beliefs,

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:26.320
the way they see the world,

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.632
will seep into us

00:00:27.632 --> 00:00:30.484
to help us become the
best version of ourselves.

00:00:30.484 --> 00:00:32.312
So today we're going to
learn from Jordan Peterson

00:00:32.312 --> 00:00:34.864
and some of his most amazing moments.

00:00:34.864 --> 00:00:36.364
Mentor me, Jordan.

00:00:48.751 --> 00:00:51.155
- My experience is with people

00:00:51.155 --> 00:00:52.344
that we're probably running

00:00:52.344 --> 00:00:55.196
at about at 51% of our capacity.

00:00:55.196 --> 00:00:57.282
So, you can think about this yourselves.

00:00:57.282 --> 00:01:01.537
I often ask undergraduates,
how many hours a day you waste,

00:01:01.537 --> 00:01:02.970
or how many hours a week you waste.

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:04.871
The classic answer is something like

00:01:04.871 --> 00:01:06.946
four to six hours a day.

00:01:06.946 --> 00:01:08.641
Ya know, inefficient studying,

00:01:08.641 --> 00:01:10.827
watching things on YouTube,

00:01:10.827 --> 00:01:12.529
that not only do you not want to watch

00:01:12.529 --> 00:01:13.668
that you don't even care about

00:01:13.668 --> 00:01:16.301
that make you feel horrible
about watching after your done.

00:01:16.301 --> 00:01:18.852
That's probably four hours right there.

00:01:18.852 --> 00:01:22.347
Now, you think, well
that's 25 hours a week,

00:01:22.347 --> 00:01:25.586
it's 100 hours a month,
that's 2 1/2 full work weeks,

00:01:25.586 --> 00:01:28.596
it's half a year of work weeks per year.

00:01:28.596 --> 00:01:31.536
And if your time is worth $20 an hour,

00:01:31.536 --> 00:01:33.014
which is a radical underestimate.

00:01:33.014 --> 00:01:35.217
It's probably more like
$50 if you think about it

00:01:35.217 --> 00:01:36.984
in terms of deferred wages.

00:01:36.984 --> 00:01:39.289
If you're wasting 20 hours a week,

00:01:39.289 --> 00:01:41.779
you're wasting $50,000 a year.

00:01:41.779 --> 00:01:43.614
And you are doing that right now.

00:01:43.614 --> 00:01:46.349
It's because you're young,
wasting $50,000 a year

00:01:46.349 --> 00:01:48.321
is a way bigger catastrophe
than it would be

00:01:48.321 --> 00:01:49.305
for me to waste it,

00:01:49.305 --> 00:01:51.389
because I'm not going
to last nearly as long.

00:01:51.389 --> 00:01:53.259
And so if your life isn't
everything it could be,

00:01:53.259 --> 00:01:54.213
you could ask yourself,

00:01:54.213 --> 00:01:56.620
well, what would happen if
you just stopped wasting

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:59.850
the opportunities that
are in front of you?

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:02.097
You'd be who knows how
much more efficient,

00:02:02.097 --> 00:02:06.015
ten times more efficient,
20 times more efficient.

00:02:06.015 --> 00:02:07.854
That's the Pareto distribution.

00:02:07.854 --> 00:02:11.147
You have no idea how efficient
efficient people get.

00:02:11.147 --> 00:02:13.730
It's completely off the charts.

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:18.513
Well, and if we all got our
act together collectively,

00:02:18.513 --> 00:02:20.330
and stopped making things worse,

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.536
because that's another thing
people do all the time.

00:02:22.536 --> 00:02:24.745
Not only do they not do what they should

00:02:24.745 --> 00:02:26.093
to make things better,

00:02:26.093 --> 00:02:27.920
they actively attempt to make things worse

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.740
because they're spiteful or resentful

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:31.528
or arrogant or deceitful

00:02:31.528 --> 00:02:33.695
or homicidal or genocidal,

00:02:34.767 --> 00:02:38.232
or all of those things
all bundled together,

00:02:38.232 --> 00:02:40.508
in an absolutely pathological package.

00:02:40.508 --> 00:02:43.118
If people stopped really, really trying

00:02:43.118 --> 00:02:44.621
just to make things worse,

00:02:44.621 --> 00:02:46.468
we have no idea how much
better they would get

00:02:46.468 --> 00:02:48.135
just because of that.

00:02:48.135 --> 00:02:50.606
You are actually tougher than you think.

00:02:50.606 --> 00:02:51.996
You never knew that.

00:02:51.996 --> 00:02:54.141
And maybe you didn't want to
take on the responsibility.

00:02:54.141 --> 00:02:57.250
Because, you know, people play
a role in their own demise,

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:58.244
so to speak.

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:00.955
When you had an opportunity
to go out and explore

00:03:00.955 --> 00:03:03.079
or withdraw because you were afraid,

00:03:03.079 --> 00:03:05.722
you chose to withdraw
because you were afraid.

00:03:05.722 --> 00:03:08.027
So it's not only that you
were over-protected often,

00:03:08.027 --> 00:03:09.784
it's that you were
willing to take advantage

00:03:09.784 --> 00:03:12.366
of the fact that you were overprotected

00:03:12.366 --> 00:03:15.461
and run back there whenever
you had the opportunity.

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:17.242
So maybe you're a kid in the playground,

00:03:17.242 --> 00:03:19.546
and you're having trouble with other kids,

00:03:19.546 --> 00:03:20.940
and you know in the back of your mind,

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.679
I should deal with this myself.

00:03:23.679 --> 00:03:25.967
But you go and tell you mom
and get her to intervene.

00:03:25.967 --> 00:03:27.761
And you know that that's not right.

00:03:27.761 --> 00:03:29.674
You know that you're
breaking the social contract,

00:03:29.674 --> 00:03:31.116
but it's easier,

00:03:31.116 --> 00:03:32.396
so that's what you do.

00:03:32.396 --> 00:03:34.033
You run off to an authority figure,

00:03:34.033 --> 00:03:37.650
and hide behind the great
father, roughly speaking.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:38.483
Well, the problem with that

00:03:38.483 --> 00:03:40.120
is you don't learn how to do it yourself.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:43.716
So then you have to relearn
it painfully when you're 40.

00:03:43.716 --> 00:03:46.441
So then you take people out, and you say,

00:03:46.441 --> 00:03:48.473
what are you afraid of?

00:03:48.473 --> 00:03:50.534
Rank it from one to ten.

00:03:50.534 --> 00:03:53.602
So ten, make a list of ten
things you're afraid of.

00:03:53.602 --> 00:03:56.957
The thing you're least afraid
of, we'll call number ten.

00:03:56.957 --> 00:03:58.316
So we'll start with that.

00:03:58.316 --> 00:03:59.854
Okay, well I'm afraid of elevators.

00:03:59.854 --> 00:04:02.617
Okay, well let's look at
a picture of an elevator.

00:04:02.617 --> 00:04:05.583
Let's have you imagine
being in an elevator.

00:04:05.583 --> 00:04:07.553
Let's go up to an elevator and let you

00:04:07.553 --> 00:04:10.001
watch the terrible jaws of death open,

00:04:10.001 --> 00:04:12.935
because that's how you
respond to it symbolically.

00:04:12.935 --> 00:04:14.397
Right? And you're going to do that

00:04:14.397 --> 00:04:17.730
at the closest proximity you can manage.

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:21.823
You find out you go do that, it works.

00:04:21.823 --> 00:04:22.656
You're nervous as hell,

00:04:22.656 --> 00:04:26.670
especially from an anticipatory
perspective. Shaking.

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:29.291
You go out, you stop, you watch it happen,

00:04:29.291 --> 00:04:30.315
and you actually calm down.

00:04:30.315 --> 00:04:31.259
You do that ten times,

00:04:31.259 --> 00:04:32.867
it no longer bothers you.

00:04:32.867 --> 00:04:34.949
Well, what you've learned
that you didn't die,

00:04:34.949 --> 00:04:36.805
but more importantly than that,

00:04:36.805 --> 00:04:40.428
you've learned that you can
withstand the threat of death.

00:04:40.428 --> 00:04:41.787
That's what you've learned.

00:04:41.787 --> 00:04:43.374
Then you move a little closer,

00:04:43.374 --> 00:04:44.207
and then you move a little closer,

00:04:44.207 --> 00:04:45.040
and then you move a little closer,

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.298
and finally you're back in
what's no longer the elevator

00:04:48.298 --> 00:04:49.793
from a symbolic perspective.

00:04:49.793 --> 00:04:51.145
It's a tube.

00:04:51.145 --> 00:04:54.785
It's a place of enclosure and isolation.

00:04:54.785 --> 00:04:58.506
And you learn, it turns
out I can withstand that.

00:04:58.506 --> 00:05:01.260
And then you're much more together,

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:02.686
much more confident.

00:05:02.686 --> 00:05:04.950
And that's often one of the
things that often happens

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:06.089
in situations like that.

00:05:06.089 --> 00:05:07.277
I've seen this multiple times, is that

00:05:07.277 --> 00:05:10.070
If you run someone through

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:14.084
an exposure training process
like that and toughen them up,

00:05:14.084 --> 00:05:16.468
they'll often start standing
up to people around them

00:05:16.468 --> 00:05:18.154
in a way they never did before.

00:05:18.154 --> 00:05:21.792
Stop doing the things
that you know are wrong,

00:05:21.792 --> 00:05:24.416
that you couldn't stop doing.

00:05:24.416 --> 00:05:27.362
Right? So, it's a fairly limited attempt.

00:05:27.362 --> 00:05:29.302
First of all, we're not
going to say that you know

00:05:29.302 --> 00:05:31.406
what the good is or what the truth is

00:05:31.406 --> 00:05:33.022
in any ultimate sense.

00:05:33.022 --> 00:05:35.571
But we will presume that
there are things you're doing

00:05:35.571 --> 00:05:36.903
that for one reason or another you know

00:05:36.903 --> 00:05:38.212
are not in your best interest.

00:05:38.212 --> 00:05:39.226
There's something about them

00:05:39.226 --> 00:05:41.319
that you just know you should just stop.

00:05:41.319 --> 00:05:43.095
They're kind of self-evident to you.

00:05:43.095 --> 00:05:45.199
Other things you're going
to be doubtful about,

00:05:45.199 --> 00:05:49.698
you're not going to know which
way is up and which way is down.

00:05:49.698 --> 00:05:50.847
But there are things that you're doing

00:05:50.847 --> 00:05:52.502
that you know you shouldn't do.

00:05:52.502 --> 00:05:55.886
Now some of those you won't
stop doing, for whatever reason.

00:05:55.886 --> 00:05:57.053
You don't have the discipline,

00:05:57.053 --> 00:05:58.568
or maybe there's a secondary payoff,

00:05:58.568 --> 00:05:59.970
or you don't believe it's necessary,

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:01.462
or it's too much of a sacrifice,

00:06:01.462 --> 00:06:04.428
or you're angry or resentful, or afraid.

00:06:04.428 --> 00:06:07.142
Who knows? So forget about those for now.

00:06:07.142 --> 00:06:10.175
But there's another subset
that you could stop doing.

00:06:10.175 --> 00:06:11.539
It might be little things.

00:06:11.539 --> 00:06:12.547
Well, that's fine.

00:06:12.547 --> 00:06:15.072
Stop doing it, and see what happens.

00:06:15.072 --> 00:06:16.558
And what will happen is,

00:06:16.558 --> 00:06:18.853
you're vision will clear a little bit.

00:06:18.853 --> 00:06:20.998
And then something else will pop up,

00:06:20.998 --> 00:06:23.240
and you feel the apprehension

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.367
that you will also know
you should stop doing,

00:06:25.367 --> 00:06:27.053
and that you could stop doing,

00:06:27.053 --> 00:06:28.493
because you strengthened yourself a bit

00:06:28.493 --> 00:06:30.324
by stopping doing the
particular stupid thing

00:06:30.324 --> 00:06:31.604
that you were doing before.

00:06:31.604 --> 00:06:33.401
That just puts you
together a little bit more.

00:06:33.401 --> 00:06:36.745
And you could do that repeatedly for

00:06:36.745 --> 00:06:38.917
an indefinite period of time.

00:06:38.917 --> 00:06:41.682
And that doesn't necessarily mean

00:06:41.682 --> 00:06:43.792
that you're going to
ever be able to formulate

00:06:43.792 --> 00:06:45.754
a clear and final picture

00:06:45.754 --> 00:06:48.952
of what constitutes
the truth and the good.

00:06:48.952 --> 00:06:51.199
But it does mean that you'll
be able to continually

00:06:51.199 --> 00:06:54.453
move away from what's
untrue and what's bad.

00:06:54.453 --> 00:06:57.345
And you know, that's not a bad start.

00:06:57.345 --> 00:06:59.449
No one can live without a routine.

00:06:59.449 --> 00:07:00.446
Just forget that.

00:07:00.446 --> 00:07:02.824
If you guys don't have a routine,

00:07:02.824 --> 00:07:05.143
I would recommend that you get one going,

00:07:05.143 --> 00:07:07.650
because you cannot be mentally healthy

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:09.010
without a routine.

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:11.035
You need to pick a time to get up,

00:07:11.035 --> 00:07:12.985
whatever time you want, but pick one

00:07:12.985 --> 00:07:14.274
and stick to it, because otherwise,

00:07:14.274 --> 00:07:17.525
you dis-regulate your circadian rhythms,

00:07:17.525 --> 00:07:20.421
and they regulate your mood.

00:07:20.421 --> 00:07:22.645
And eat something in the morning.

00:07:22.645 --> 00:07:25.814
I have lots of clients who
have anxiety disorders.

00:07:25.814 --> 00:07:28.447
I had one client who
was literally starving,

00:07:28.447 --> 00:07:30.174
very smart girl.

00:07:30.174 --> 00:07:31.941
There was very little that she liked.

00:07:31.941 --> 00:07:33.575
She kind of tried to subsist on

00:07:33.575 --> 00:07:35.192
half a cup of rice a day.

00:07:35.192 --> 00:07:36.440
She came to me and said,

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:37.813
I have no energy. I come home.

00:07:37.813 --> 00:07:40.576
All I want to do is watch
the same movie over and over.

00:07:40.576 --> 00:07:41.743
Is that weird?

00:07:42.668 --> 00:07:45.362
And I thought, it depends
on how hard you work.

00:07:45.362 --> 00:07:48.358
It's a little weird, but whatever.

00:07:48.358 --> 00:07:50.713
It's familiar, and you're
looking for comfort.

00:07:50.713 --> 00:07:53.002
So I did an analysis of her diet,

00:07:53.002 --> 00:07:56.960
and it's like 3/4 cup of rice.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:59.002
It's like, you're starving. Eat something.

00:07:59.002 --> 00:08:01.335
Ya know, you'll feel better.

00:08:03.465 --> 00:08:05.632
So, she modified her diet,

00:08:06.634 --> 00:08:07.976
and all of her anxiety went away,

00:08:07.976 --> 00:08:09.500
and she had some energy.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:12.250
It was like, yeah, you got to eat.

00:08:14.051 --> 00:08:17.818
So, a schedule. That's a good thing, man.

00:08:17.818 --> 00:08:19.516
Your brain will thank you for it,

00:08:19.516 --> 00:08:22.044
It will stabilize your nervous system.

00:08:22.044 --> 00:08:24.047
When it's a bit of a
plan, that's a good thing.

00:08:24.047 --> 00:08:26.346
Well if you're harmless,
you're not virtuous.

00:08:26.346 --> 00:08:28.688
You're just harmless.

00:08:28.688 --> 00:08:30.474
You're like a rabbit.

00:08:30.474 --> 00:08:32.052
A rabbit isn't virtuous.

00:08:32.052 --> 00:08:36.203
It just can't do anything
except get eaten.

00:08:36.203 --> 00:08:37.883
It's not virtuous.

00:08:37.883 --> 00:08:41.854
If you're a monster, and
you don't act monstrously,

00:08:41.854 --> 00:08:43.441
then you're virtuous.

00:08:43.441 --> 00:08:45.046
But you also have to be a monster.

00:08:45.046 --> 00:08:46.509
Well, you see this all the time.

00:08:46.509 --> 00:08:47.667
Harry Potter's like that, too.

00:08:47.667 --> 00:08:49.486
It's like he's flawed, he's hurt,

00:08:49.486 --> 00:08:51.702
he's got evil in him, he
can talk to snakes, man.

00:08:51.702 --> 00:08:54.485
He breaks rules all the time.

00:08:54.485 --> 00:08:55.388
All the time.

00:08:55.388 --> 00:08:57.460
He's not obedient at all.

00:08:57.460 --> 00:08:59.858
But, you know, he has a good
reason for breaking the rules.

00:08:59.858 --> 00:09:02.468
And if he couldn't break the rules,

00:09:02.468 --> 00:09:05.587
him and his little clique of
rule-breaking trouble-makers,

00:09:05.587 --> 00:09:07.863
if they didn't break the rules,

00:09:07.863 --> 00:09:10.252
they wouldn't obtain the highest goal.

00:09:10.252 --> 00:09:11.755
So it's very peculiar,

00:09:11.755 --> 00:09:15.922
but it's a very, very, very
common mythological notion.

00:09:17.453 --> 00:09:21.536
The hero has to be, the
hero has to be a monster,

00:09:23.377 --> 00:09:24.909
but a controlled monster.

00:09:24.909 --> 00:09:26.169
Batman is like that.

00:09:26.169 --> 00:09:29.756
Ya know, it's everywhere, it's
the story you always hear.

00:09:29.756 --> 00:09:31.605
I started to pay very careful attention

00:09:31.605 --> 00:09:33.779
to what I was saying.

00:09:33.779 --> 00:09:36.615
I don't know if that happened
voluntarily or involuntarily,

00:09:36.615 --> 00:09:38.493
but I could feel a sort of split

00:09:38.493 --> 00:09:39.437
developing in my psyche.

00:09:39.437 --> 00:09:41.918
And this split, and I've
actually had students tell me

00:09:41.918 --> 00:09:44.072
the same thing has happened to them

00:09:44.072 --> 00:09:46.914
after they've listened
to some of the material

00:09:46.914 --> 00:09:50.126
that I've been describing to all of you.

00:09:50.126 --> 00:09:53.126
But, it's split into two, let's say,

00:09:55.156 --> 00:09:57.127
and one part was the, let's say,

00:09:57.127 --> 00:10:00.041
the old me that was talking a lot,

00:10:00.041 --> 00:10:04.076
and that liked to argue
and that liked ideas,

00:10:04.076 --> 00:10:07.062
and there was another part
that was watching that part,

00:10:07.062 --> 00:10:11.562
just with its eyes open,
and neutrally judging,

00:10:11.562 --> 00:10:13.706
and the part that was neutrally judging,

00:10:13.706 --> 00:10:16.330
was watching the part that
was talking and going,

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:18.080
that isn't your idea.

00:10:19.041 --> 00:10:20.971
You don't really believe that.

00:10:20.971 --> 00:10:23.096
You don't really know
what you're talking about.

00:10:23.096 --> 00:10:24.689
That isn't true.

00:10:24.689 --> 00:10:27.125
And I thought, hmmm,
that's really interesting.

00:10:27.125 --> 00:10:29.173
And that was happening to like 95%

00:10:29.173 --> 00:10:30.735
of what I was saying,

00:10:30.735 --> 00:10:31.960
so then I didn't really know what to do.

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:33.365
I thought, okay, this is strange.

00:10:33.365 --> 00:10:37.050
So maybe I fragmented enough,
just not a good thing at all.

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:39.123
It wasn't like I was hearing voices

00:10:39.123 --> 00:10:40.577
or anything like that.

00:10:40.577 --> 00:10:42.415
It wasn't like that.

00:10:42.415 --> 00:10:45.832
It was, well, people have multiple parts.

00:10:46.951 --> 00:10:48.687
So then I had this weird conundrum,

00:10:48.687 --> 00:10:52.742
and I was like, well which
of these two things are me?

00:10:52.742 --> 00:10:54.326
Is it the part that listening and saying,

00:10:54.326 --> 00:10:57.076
no, that's rubbish, that's a lie.

00:10:58.481 --> 00:11:00.451
You're doing that to impress people.

00:11:00.451 --> 00:11:02.928
You're just trying to win
the argument, ya know.

00:11:02.928 --> 00:11:04.483
Was that me or was the
part that was going about

00:11:04.483 --> 00:11:06.900
my normal verbal business me?

00:11:08.131 --> 00:11:09.373
And I didn't know, but I decided

00:11:09.373 --> 00:11:10.958
I would go with the critic.

00:11:10.958 --> 00:11:12.990
Then what I tried to do,

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:14.666
what I learned to do, I think,

00:11:14.666 --> 00:11:18.333
was to stop saying
things that made me weak.

00:11:19.288 --> 00:11:21.819
I mean, I'm still trying to do that,

00:11:21.819 --> 00:11:24.916
because I'm always
feeling that when I talk,

00:11:24.916 --> 00:11:27.223
whether or not the words I'm saying

00:11:27.223 --> 00:11:30.718
are either making me align or come apart.

00:11:30.718 --> 00:11:33.043
And I think the alignment,

00:11:33.043 --> 00:11:35.574
I really do think
alignment is the right way

00:11:35.574 --> 00:11:36.598
of conceptualizing it.

00:11:36.598 --> 00:11:37.727
Because I think if you say things

00:11:37.727 --> 00:11:41.709
that are as true as you
can say them, let's say,

00:11:41.709 --> 00:11:43.209
then they come up,

00:11:44.325 --> 00:11:47.129
they come out of the depths inside of you,

00:11:47.129 --> 00:11:48.449
because we don't know
where thoughts come from.

00:11:48.449 --> 00:11:51.325
We don't know how far down
into your sub-structure

00:11:51.325 --> 00:11:52.443
that thoughts emerge.

00:11:52.443 --> 00:11:55.256
We don't know what processes
of physiological alignment

00:11:55.256 --> 00:11:57.257
are necessary for you to speak

00:11:57.257 --> 00:11:59.074
from the core of your being.

00:11:59.074 --> 00:12:00.285
We don't understand any of that.

00:12:00.285 --> 00:12:01.485
We don't even conceptualize that.

00:12:01.485 --> 00:12:02.883
But I believe that you can feel that.

00:12:02.883 --> 00:12:04.030
And I learned some of that

00:12:04.030 --> 00:12:05.552
from reading Carl Rogers, by the way,

00:12:05.552 --> 00:12:07.281
who's a great clinician.

00:12:07.281 --> 00:12:09.324
Because he talked about
mental health, in part,

00:12:09.324 --> 00:12:13.491
as the coherence between the
spiritual or the abstract,

00:12:14.684 --> 00:12:16.158
and the physical.

00:12:16.158 --> 00:12:17.776
That the two things were aligned.

00:12:17.776 --> 00:12:19.643
And there's a lot of idea of alignment

00:12:19.643 --> 00:12:21.282
in psychoanalytic and clinical thinking.

00:12:21.282 --> 00:12:25.486
But anyways, I decided that
I would start practicing

00:12:25.486 --> 00:12:28.359
not saying things that would make me weak,

00:12:28.359 --> 00:12:30.785
and what happened was
that I had to stop saying

00:12:30.785 --> 00:12:33.589
almost everything that I was saying.

00:12:33.589 --> 00:12:35.368
I would say 95% of it.

00:12:35.368 --> 00:12:37.144
It's a hell of a shock to wake up,

00:12:37.144 --> 00:12:39.125
I mean, this was over a few months,

00:12:39.125 --> 00:12:40.720
but it's a hell of a shock to wake up

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:44.137
and realize that you're mostly dead wood.

00:12:45.007 --> 00:12:46.429
It's a shock.

00:12:46.429 --> 00:12:47.424
Ya know, and you might think,

00:12:47.424 --> 00:12:49.396
well do you really want
all of that to burn off?

00:12:49.396 --> 00:12:51.651
Well, there's nothing left
but a little husk, 5% of you.

00:12:51.651 --> 00:12:55.151
It's like, well, if that 5% is solid,

00:12:57.046 --> 00:13:00.052
then maybe that's exactly
what you want to have happen.

00:13:00.052 --> 00:13:01.110
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:02.329
I hope you enjoyed.

00:13:02.329 --> 00:13:04.576
I'd love to know what did
you learn from this video

00:13:04.576 --> 00:13:07.145
that you're going to
immediately apply somehow

00:13:07.145 --> 00:13:08.747
to your life or your business?

00:13:08.747 --> 00:13:10.109
What was the single most important lesson

00:13:10.109 --> 00:13:11.208
that you learned?

00:13:11.208 --> 00:13:12.418
Leave it in the comments below.

00:13:12.418 --> 00:13:14.200
I'm really curious to find out.

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:15.701
I also want to give a quick
shout-out to Surge Wisdom.

00:13:15.701 --> 00:13:17.977
Thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:13:17.977 --> 00:13:19.042
Your One Word,

00:13:19.042 --> 00:13:23.593
and for doing multiple reviews
on the book for each chapter.

00:13:23.593 --> 00:13:24.966
You can go check it out on this channel.

00:13:24.966 --> 00:13:27.442
I really appreciate the support,

00:13:27.442 --> 00:13:30.525
and I'm so glad you enjoyed the book.

00:13:38.001 --> 00:13:39.118
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:13:39.118 --> 00:13:40.529
I believe in you,

00:13:40.529 --> 00:13:42.601
and I hope you continue
to believe in yourself

00:13:42.601 --> 00:13:44.613
and whatever your one word is,

00:13:44.613 --> 00:13:47.363
much love, and I'll see you soon.

